WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.139
Transactions are big: almost thirteen million rows for one week.

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.761
And most questions ask about a day or two.

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:12.609
So we partition the table by date.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:18.606
Every day gets its own folder of files, and a query for one day only opens that day's files.

00:00:19.556 --> 00:00:24.971
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: create raw dot transactions, with the columns we need.

00:00:25.252 --> 00:00:27.500
We leave out input, the largest column.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.132
Then set it partitioned by date, before any data goes in.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:38.579
Now load the week, the first to the seventh of October, straight from S3.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:43.122
That is about nine hundred megabytes to download, so it takes a few minutes.

00:00:43.402 --> 00:00:44.652
The video speeds it up.

00:05:20.993 --> 00:05:24.179
Transactions per day: between one and a half and two million.

00:05:24.459 --> 00:05:26.422
Twelve point seven million in total.

00:05:27.372 --> 00:05:33.534
On disk, under raw transactions, there is now one folder per date, with one Parquet file in each.

00:05:34.484 --> 00:05:39.363
A query for one day, the third of October, only needs the file in that day's folder.

00:05:39.643 --> 00:05:43.892
The catalog knows which file holds which date, so the rest is never opened.

00:05:44.842 --> 00:05:47.506
In Data Lake, open the transactions table.

00:05:47.786 --> 00:05:49.918
The Partitions tab shows the key: date.

00:06:01.603 --> 00:06:06.353
Choose a key that your queries filter on, with not too many values: a day, or a month.

00:06:06.633 --> 00:06:10.851
Partition by something like an address, and you get millions of tiny files.

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:13.257
You can change the key later.

00:06:13.537 --> 00:06:17.766
The new key applies to new data only; existing files stay as they are.

00:06:18.716 --> 00:06:23.494
Save the script as 4 underscore 1 partitioning dot sequel, commit, and push.

00:06:46.751 --> 00:06:54.288
To recap: raw transactions is partitioned by date, with one folder per day, so a query for one day opens one file.

00:06:54.568 --> 00:06:58.714
Next: statistics, and how they skip files even without partitions.
